  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The residual lies in [-0.1, 0.1], so its direct effect on a pairwise score difference is at most 0.2. Its scale relative to baseline candidate-score gaps is unmeasured; training margins against individual negatives cannot establish whether it can change top-10 rankings.",
    "Registering an unused attention table in the pooling control matches stored parameters, not effective capacity or computation. Tying candidate queries and history keys also imposes symmetric attention similarity, although the value/target transition score remains asymmetric.",
    "Reproduction requires explicit initialization, normalization epsilon, branch regularization, optimizer groups, all-padding softmax behavior, and scoring dispatch. Tensor shapes alone cannot distinguish aligned B×L targets from B×C candidates when C=L.",
    "Baseline timing does not establish the candidate-attention configuration's feasible epoch cap. Candidate-dependent training costs, checkpoint retention, and a deterministic fallback for budget exhaustion need specification.",
    "The report omits paired validation uncertainty and selection optimism. Sparse independent embedding tables may overfit frequent transitions, and ID-only attention cannot establish semantic specificity. The proposed ratings remain subjective; implementation difficulty may be understated."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Assess whether the fixed bounded residual can meaningfully change rankings and identify initialization and scaling practices. Actual score-gap measurements must come from the permitted trial.",
      "query": "In SASRec augmented with an additive cosine-normalized residual alpha*r(user,item), where r is in [-1,1] and alpha=0.1, what methods calibrate residual scale against unnormalized SASRec logits and top-k boundary gaps while preserving useful gradients? Look for sequential recommendation implementations with explicit initialization and residual scaling."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Strengthen the novelty assessment and identify controls that distinguish candidate conditioning from additional trainable capacity and transition memorization.",
      "query": "Compare candidate-conditioned history attention in DIN, DARE, FPMC, and FOSSIL with a SASRec residual using a shared query/key item table and separate source-value and target tables over the last ten interactions. What prior mechanisms, sparse-item regularization, and effective-capacity ablations are relevant?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Improve evaluation reporting without changing the pinned evaluator or treating sampled ranking gains as full-catalog evidence.",
      "query": "For sequential recommendation model selection using fixed sampled HR@10 with 200 users per dataset, 99 unique negatives, and four equally weighted datasets, what are the limitations of sampled metrics, paired stratified bootstrap confidence intervals, and selecting the best of three configurations on one validation set?"
    }
  ]
